{"items":[{"targetId":"n0000229","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"At s=1, a skew-symmetric generator has only d(d−1)/2 independent entries, because its diagonal is zero and each off-diagonal pair is determined by one value.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why the s=1 parameterization uses fewer parameters."}]},{"targetId":"n0000231","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A Kronecker product constructs a large d-dimensional connection from smaller factors, replacing an unconstrained dense interaction with structured factorwise interactions.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains how imposed symmetry yields subquadratic scaling."}]},{"targetId":"n0000238","injections":[{"type":"transition","text":"KromHC can reduce the cost of structured residual mixing while go-mHC controls the expressivity–efficiency trade-off of the constituent doubly stochastic maps.","densityScore":0.72,"relation":"supports","reason":"States the distinct roles that make the methods composable."}]},{"targetId":"n0000240","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Increasing the size of each Kronecker factor enlarges the family of representable residual maps, while the factor representation grows polynomially rather than requiring one weight per permutation.","densityScore":0.75,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Connects larger products to recovered expressivity and quadratic cost."}]},{"targetId":"n0000242","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The comparison replaces factorial dependence on factor size with quadratic dependence, so increasing i_k is much more tractable than enumerating all i_k! permutation components.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Makes the asymptotic improvement operationally meaningful."}]},{"targetId":"n0000246","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"An 8% FLOP increase can remain consequential at 27B parameters because it is incurred repeatedly across training and inference workloads.","densityScore":0.69,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Provides practical context for the reported overhead."}]},{"targetId":"n0000247","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Iterative projection updates require repeated normalization or correction steps during each forward computation; removing them leaves a direct parameterized map evaluation.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the stated source of the speed-up."}]},{"targetId":"n0000250","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Matching asymptotic orders does not imply equal runtime: constants, memory traffic, and parallelization of the direct go-mHC computation can still determine measured latency.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"Qualifies the FLOP-order comparison without changing the claim."}]},{"targetId":"n0000251","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"A useful residual-stream parameterization must improve optimization or model quality enough to justify the additional mixing computation at the intended stream width.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"supports","reason":"Makes explicit the performance-versus-cost criterion behind the middle-ground goal."}]},{"targetId":"n0000252","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The stated constants define one operating point, so the claimed middle ground should be interpreted as configuration-dependent rather than a universal ordering for every stream count and factor size.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"supports","reason":"Clarifies the scope of the complexity comparison."}]},{"targetId":"n0000258","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Full connectivity means that, within the parameterized manifold, there is a continuous parameter path to a representation of any admissible target matrix.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Defines a central property used by the convergence claim."}]},{"targetId":"n0000259","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"When the parameter-to-matrix map becomes locally flat, gradient descent receives little signal to move toward a different residual transform even if that transform is representable.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains how vanishing gradients obstruct relearning."}]},{"targetId":"n0000261","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Because T_i is known when Y_{i,j} is generated, reconstruction error can directly measure how closely a learned parameterization recovers the intended residual-stream transformation under noise.","densityScore":0.87,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why the synthetic setup evaluates reconstruction capability."}]},{"targetId":"split-000010","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Birkhoff polytope is the set of square doubly stochastic matrices: nonnegative matrices whose rows and columns each sum to one.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"enables","reason":"Supplies the prerequisite meaning of the approximated set."}]},{"targetId":"split-000015","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The residual hyper-connection mixes stream states before the layer contribution is added, so it changes how information is routed across streams at every layer.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the role of the matrix term in the update equation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000062","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A generalized orthostochastic construction derives a doubly stochastic matrix from structured orthogonal components, preserving stochastic constraints by construction rather than by iterative projection.","densityScore":0.7,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Provides a functional definition of the named parameterization."}]},{"targetId":"split-000064","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The hyperparameter s acts as a capacity knob: smaller values restrict the reachable matrix family for efficiency, while larger values enlarge it toward full expressivity.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Makes the interpolation claim concrete."}]},{"targetId":"split-000065","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Parameterization geometry determines which matrices are reachable and how gradients traverse them, so it can affect both representational capacity and optimization conditioning.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Connects geometry to performance and stability."}]},{"targetId":"split-000068","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Exact coverage of all doubly stochastic matrices can demand a large or costly parameterization, whereas a scalable restricted family sacrifices some reachable transformations.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"States the source of the exactness–scalability trade-off."}]},{"targetId":"split-000069","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The parameter count d(d−1)/2 equals the number of independent entries in a d×d skew-symmetric matrix.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the origin of the stated parameter count."}]},{"targetId":"split-000070","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"There are d! permutation matrices of size d, so assigning an independent mixture weight to each one produces factorial parameter growth.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why full coverage has factorial cost."}]},{"targetId":"split-000074","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"If the residual state contains multiple streams, the hyper-connection matrix operates across the stream dimension while Layer F supplies the usual layer transformation.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies the equation's stream-level interpretation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000087","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Birkhoff–von Neumann theorem states that every doubly stochastic matrix can be expressed as a convex combination of permutation matrices.","densityScore":0.94,"relation":"enables","reason":"Supplies the theorem required to understand the construction."}]},{"targetId":"split-000090","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"If smaller factors are exactly doubly stochastic, their Kronecker product is also doubly stochastic, allowing large structured residual maps to inherit the constraint.","densityScore":0.89,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains how KromHC preserves exact stochasticity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000098","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The ordered product H_{l→L}^{res} represents the cumulative linear routing effect of residual hyper-connections from layer l through layer L.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the meaning of the product quantity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000099","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The Karpelevič region characterizes eigenvalues attainable by stochastic matrices, so spectral coverage tests which long-range mixing and contraction behaviors the learned maps can realize.","densityScore":0.73,"relation":"enables","reason":"Supplies context for the spectral-region claim."}]},{"targetId":"split-000104","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Repeated products of matrices from a constrained Kronecker family can compose transformations without enlarging the family’s attainable eigenvalue region.","densityScore":0.68,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the stated invariance under additional products."}]}]}
